# GEAR subgrid radiation examples

Test cases for the GEAR subgrid radiation model: HII photoionization
(`GEARFeedback:with_photoionization`) and radiation pressure
(`GEARFeedback:radiation_pressure_efficiency`). Each subdirectory is
self-contained and carries its own README with the configure line, the run
command and how to check the result. Start there once you have picked one
below.

The model tags gas particles as ionized against a per-star photon budget
rather than transporting radiation, so the examples come in two kinds:
those that compare against an analytic solution, and those that exercise a
particular task-graph or geometry configuration where the answer is
"it runs correctly", not a number.

## Comparison against an analytic solution

| Example | Reproduces |
|---|---|
| `StromgrenSphere` | Equilibrium Stromgren radius and Spitzer (1978) D-type expansion for a single source in a uniform box. The simplest case, and the base configuration the others vary. |
| `Starbench` | The STARBENCH late-phase D-type expansion test (Bisbas et al. 2015, MNRAS 453, 1324). Uses that paper's own semi-empirical solution, not Spitzer, which over-predicts at late times. |
| `Hu2017` | Four co-located sources in a dense uniform medium (Hu et al. 2017, MNRAS 471, 2151, Appendix A; the same test appears in Smith et al. 2021). Also the setup used for cadence studies. |
| `ClumpSmith2021` | Erosion of a dense clump by a nearby source (Smith et al. 2021, MNRAS 506, 3882, Fig. 2). Its erosion measurement compares several runs, so it ships matrix-analysis scripts as well as a single-run one. |
| `StromgrenSphereCosmo` | The `StromgrenSphere` box with cosmology enabled, in two tiers: an identity tier at a ~ 1 that must reproduce the non-cosmological result, and a high-redshift tier checked against the analytic formulas at the instantaneous physical density. |
| `RadiationPressureShellExpansion` | The radiation-pressure momentum channel alone, with photoionization, cooling and gravity off. The shell it drives outward is compared against the momentum-conserving analytic solution of Krumholz & Matzner (2009, ApJ 703, 1352). |

The analytic formulas take the gas density, temperatures, ionizing photon
rate and mean molecular weight as inputs, so each check is evaluated at its
own run's parameters. A setup does not have to match a paper's exact
configuration for the comparison to be meaningful.

## Geometry and task-graph configurations

| Example | Exercises |
|---|---|
| `TaskGraphPair` | A source whose region spans a pair of top-level cells, so the neighbour search crosses a cell boundary. |
| `TaskGraphCluster` | Many co-located sources competing for gas in the same region, which exercises the locking in the star loops. |

These two have no analytic reference. A correct result is a run that
reaches `time_end` with no error in the log, plus whatever qualitative
expectation the example's own README states.

## Choosing one

`StromgrenSphere` is the place to start: it is the fastest, and every other
example is a variation on it. Use `Starbench` or
`Hu2017` to compare front expansion against the
literature, `ClumpSmith2021` for the effect on a dense clump,
and `RadiationPressureShellExpansion` if you are interested in the
momentum channel rather than ionization.

Note that the required Grackle cooling mode differs between examples.
`Starbench`, `Hu2017`, and `ClumpSmith2021` need
`--with-cooling=grackle_1`: at mode 1 and above the photon budget is
priced against the neutral hydrogen fraction from the species network
rather than against total hydrogen, which is part of the physics they
compare. The others run with any mode. Each README states which it needs.
Reconfiguring rebuilds `swift` in place, so check before you start a run
that depends on the current binary.
